+  never partway through. An iterator can run backwards when it implements `DoubleEndedIterator`,
+  whose `next_back(mut self) -> Option[Item]` gives the last item not yet given from either end
+  (with `next`, the two ends meet and no item comes twice) and whose method `rev` is, so `rev` is
+  exactly where an iterator can run backwards; `ExactSizeIterator`'s `len(self) -> int` is how many
+  items are left (ODR-091). Both are in `std.core` and are not prelude names (`[MOD-5]`). The
+  iterators of the ranges with an end and the element iterators of the views run backwards and know
+  their length; `copied`, `cloned` and `chain` run backwards when what they wrap does, and `take`,
+  `skip`, `step_by`, `enumerate` and `zip` when it also knows its length, giving the items `next`
+  would, last first; each knows its length when what it wraps does. Run backwards, `enumerate`
+  numbers its last item `start + len - 1` first, so a number past `int`'s top panics at the first
+  item.
